<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">combatpace</ansi>

A combat round is decided in an instant, but you don't read it all at
once. Its lines come to you one by one, so the fight plays out as you
watch. How fast they come is up to you:

  <ansi fg="command">set combatpace fast</ansi>     a line about every 0.4 seconds, done in 3 seconds
  <ansi fg="command">set combatpace normal</ansi>   a line about every 0.8 seconds, done in 6 seconds
  <ansi fg="command">set combatpace slow</ansi>     a line about every second, done in 7.5 seconds
  <ansi fg="command">set combatpace off</ansi>      the whole round at once

Normal is the default. If you play with a screen reader (<ansi fg="command">set</ansi> shows
ScreenReader), the default is off. <ansi fg="command">set</ansi> shows your pace.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How it plays out</ansi>

- A combat round comes every 8 seconds (<ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>), and a round's
  lines always finish before the next begins. A busy round packs its
  lines closer together to fit.
- A pause comes before a pain reaction or a death line, so the blow
  lands first. The lines listed under a spell come quickly after it.
- A companion's death notice, the closing line, and the battle summary
  come in their place, after the lines that led to them.
- Your prompt, and the web client's health bars and battle view, show
  how things stood when the round began until its lines have all come
  in. So they never tell you how a blow landed before you read it.
- If you leave the room, log out, or change your pace, anything still
  waiting comes at once. Nothing is lost.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What is never held back</ansi>

What people say, tells, and anything your own commands show you come at
once, even mid-round. You can type at any time. Only other goings-on in
the room (someone walking in, a foe choosing its next target) wait
behind the round's lines, so they don't arrive out of order.

Pacing only changes when you read the round. The fight itself is decided
the moment the round starts, the same for everyone.
